  "worked_solution": "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{\u23df}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{71} & =P{D}^{71}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{71}(\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{71} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{71}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
